WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.090
- The autopilot relies on a series

00:00:02.090 --> 00:00:05.310
of sensors all around the
aircraft collecting data.

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:07.250
- [Narrator] This is Captain Sonya Laxo,

00:00:07.250 --> 00:00:09.420
a 23-year veteran at American Airlines.

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:10.720
She, like other pilots,

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:13.380
uses autopilot on every flight,

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:15.000
and while it controls.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:18.700
- The ailerons, the
flaperons and the spoilers

00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:21.960
on both wings, the elevator on the tail

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:23.720
and then as well as the rudder.

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:26.000
- [Narrator] Autopilot isn't autonomous.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:28.410
She's here to explain
just what it actually is,

00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:30.810
how the technology behind autopilot works,

00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:33.370
and how it helps her and
her fellow pilots get you

00:00:33.370 --> 00:00:34.961
to your destination.

00:00:34.961 --> 00:00:37.544
(lively music)

00:00:39.830 --> 00:00:42.360
Quick note, Laxo flies Boeing aircraft

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:44.450
and will be using Boeing terminology

00:00:44.450 --> 00:00:48.253
but autopilot works similarly
on other commercial aircraft.

00:00:48.253 --> 00:00:49.577
- The technical name for the autopilot

00:00:49.577 --> 00:00:53.757
is the Auto Flight
Control System, the AFCS.

00:00:53.757 --> 00:00:55.680
- [Narrator] The AFCS, or autopilot,

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:57.370
is just one of an increasing number

00:00:57.370 --> 00:00:58.510
of automated technologies

00:00:58.510 --> 00:01:00.560
in the cockpit, many of which are designed

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:02.490
to prevent dangerous scenarios.

00:01:02.490 --> 00:01:05.260
The autopilot system is
there to assist pilots,

00:01:05.260 --> 00:01:07.650
letting them take their
hands off the yoke.

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:10.010
- The main components
of the autopilot system

00:01:10.010 --> 00:01:12.350
are the autopilot flight director system,

00:01:12.350 --> 00:01:14.710
and the auto throttle system,

00:01:14.710 --> 00:01:18.560
actually controlled by what
we call the mode control panel

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:21.130
in the flight management
computer, the FMC,

00:01:21.130 --> 00:01:24.330
which we, mot of the time,
just call it the box.

00:01:24.330 --> 00:01:26.310
- [Narrator] The mode
control panel and the box,

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:27.760
those are both controlled by the pilots

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:28.780
on the flight deck.

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:30.710
- By using the mode control panel,

00:01:30.710 --> 00:01:32.400
the flight management computer,

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:35.140
we command that autopilot
flight director system

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:36.370
and the auto throttle system

00:01:36.370 --> 00:01:39.650
to perform the climbs,
the cruise, the descents,

00:01:39.650 --> 00:01:41.810
the approach, and in
certain circumstances,

00:01:41.810 --> 00:01:43.830
the actual landing itself.

00:01:43.830 --> 00:01:44.750
- [Narrator] But the autopilot system

00:01:44.750 --> 00:01:46.679
extends outside the cockpit.

00:01:46.679 --> 00:01:48.340
(pensive music)

00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:51.120
- [Sonya] The autopilot
relies on a series of sensors

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:53.570
all around the autopilot collecting data.

00:01:53.570 --> 00:01:55.450
- [Narrator] GPS
information, as well as data

00:01:55.450 --> 00:01:57.800
on air speed and altitude are fed back

00:01:57.800 --> 00:01:59.320
to the computers onboard,

00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:01.120
which use the information to determine

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:03.650
if a plane is veering
from its preset route.

00:02:03.650 --> 00:02:05.050
- [Sonya] The computers
are always running.

00:02:05.050 --> 00:02:06.560
I mean they're very fast speeds.

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:08.620
So any time there's
any little discrepancy,

00:02:08.620 --> 00:02:09.860
it's gonna make an adjustment

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:12.670
to keep that aircraft
on that flight profile.

00:02:12.670 --> 00:02:14.970
- That's the auto part,
the system's ability

00:02:14.970 --> 00:02:17.510
to make those adjustments
to maintain level flight

00:02:17.510 --> 00:02:20.300
along a path without pilot input.

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:23.000
- The autopilot's actually
controlling the flight controls.

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:26.067
So it's gonna be the
ailerons, the flaperons,

00:02:26.067 --> 00:02:28.400
and the spoilers on both wings,

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:31.560
the elevator on the tail,
as well as the rudder.

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:32.950
- By adjusting those components,

00:02:32.950 --> 00:02:35.780
the autopilot is able to
move the plane along one

00:02:35.780 --> 00:02:37.040
of three axes.

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:39.810
- We have the longitudinal
axes from nose to tail,

00:02:39.810 --> 00:02:41.950
the lateral from wing tip to wing tip,

00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:43.423
and then the vertical,

00:02:43.423 --> 00:02:45.590
which goes straight down
the middle of the aircraft.

00:02:45.590 --> 00:02:49.310
So when the aircraft is rotating
on the longitudinal axis,

00:02:49.310 --> 00:02:50.884
it's using the ailerons,

00:02:50.884 --> 00:02:52.710
the flaperons and spoilers to do that

00:02:52.710 --> 00:02:54.130
and then the lateral axis,

00:02:54.130 --> 00:02:56.530
that's using the elevators.

00:02:56.530 --> 00:03:00.310
And then your rudder will
be along the vertical axis.

00:03:00.310 --> 00:03:02.890
- [Narrator] While autopilot
can move the plane itself,

00:03:02.890 --> 00:03:05.090
it's not doing so without supervision.

00:03:05.090 --> 00:03:07.760
- The autopilot is a tool.

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:09.650
The autopilot doesn't fly the airplane,

00:03:09.650 --> 00:03:13.930
we fly the airplane through the
automation of the autopilot.

00:03:13.930 --> 00:03:18.080
So while the autopilot is
maintaining the wings level,

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:19.364
flying the route,

00:03:19.364 --> 00:03:21.003
the pilot is always
monitoring the autopilot.

00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:26.700
- [Narrator] This relationship
can go both ways, however.

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:28.870
Certain autopilot systems prevent input

00:03:28.870 --> 00:03:30.990
from pilots that would, for example,

00:03:30.990 --> 00:03:32.860
force the plane to turn too sharply

00:03:32.860 --> 00:03:35.233
or raise the nose at too steep an angle.

00:03:36.490 --> 00:03:39.150
To understand just how
a pilot uses autopilot,

00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:40.730
let's follow a flight's path,

00:03:40.730 --> 00:03:42.250
starting at the gate.

00:03:42.250 --> 00:03:43.760
- Initially at the gate,

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:47.710
we download the route
into the FMC, the box,

00:03:47.710 --> 00:03:50.210
so then the autopilot knows what it needs

00:03:50.210 --> 00:03:52.320
to be following as per the route

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:55.840
and also performance,
airspeed, fuel consumption.

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:58.370
- [Narrator] The autopilot
isn't engaged as yet.

00:03:58.370 --> 00:04:00.270
The pilots control the takeoff

00:04:00.270 --> 00:04:04.070
but they must engage the
autopilot above 28,000 feet due

00:04:04.070 --> 00:04:05.980
to vertical separation requirements,

00:04:05.980 --> 00:04:08.190
though most pilots
press the button sooner,

00:04:08.190 --> 00:04:10.570
letting the autopilot take control.

00:04:10.570 --> 00:04:13.280
- Analogy would be the
cruise control in your car.

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:15.150
So it assists the driver

00:04:15.150 --> 00:04:16.530
but the driver still has to be aware

00:04:16.530 --> 00:04:19.030
of their environment and
react to that environment.

00:04:19.030 --> 00:04:20.270
Just like on the flight deck,

00:04:20.270 --> 00:04:22.910
we can disconnect the
autopilot at any and all time

00:04:22.910 --> 00:04:24.950
and have full control of the aircraft.

00:04:24.950 --> 00:04:26.810
- [Narrator] But that isn't common.

00:04:26.810 --> 00:04:28.587
Pilots typically only spend a few minutes

00:04:28.587 --> 00:04:31.030
per trip manually flying the plane,

00:04:31.030 --> 00:04:33.540
though they are required to
do regular proficiency checks

00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:34.730
in a flight simulator

00:04:34.730 --> 00:04:36.700
during which certain
segments of the flight

00:04:36.700 --> 00:04:38.260
must be hand flown.

00:04:38.260 --> 00:04:41.890
Once autopilot is engaged,
pilots' jobs don't stop.

00:04:41.890 --> 00:04:43.840
- We're constantly working on our tasks

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:46.270
to ensure the safety of our passengers,

00:04:46.270 --> 00:04:48.410
getting from point A to point B.

00:04:48.410 --> 00:04:50.700
We're not sitting up here
reading the newspaper.

00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:52.220
We don't have time for that.

00:04:52.220 --> 00:04:54.340
- [Narrator] Instead, pilots
are monitoring the fuel,

00:04:54.340 --> 00:04:56.270
planning for emergencies and looking out

00:04:56.270 --> 00:04:57.190
for weather ahead,

00:04:57.190 --> 00:04:59.330
which could prompt them to
change their instructions

00:04:59.330 --> 00:05:00.630
for the autopilot.

00:05:00.630 --> 00:05:03.730
- We can use it to either
go around the weather,

00:05:03.730 --> 00:05:05.320
either using a heading mode

00:05:05.320 --> 00:05:08.320
or actually making changes
into the route itself

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:09.740
to go around the weather.

00:05:09.740 --> 00:05:11.730
So the one thing is we're always aviating,

00:05:11.730 --> 00:05:14.810
navigating, communicating but with safety

00:05:14.810 --> 00:05:16.820
being our number one priority.

00:05:16.820 --> 00:05:18.070
- [Narrator] When it comes time to land,

00:05:18.070 --> 00:05:20.140
pilots generally disengage autopilot,

00:05:20.140 --> 00:05:21.420
and land themselves

00:05:21.420 --> 00:05:24.060
but in certain types of
weather with low visibility,

00:05:24.060 --> 00:05:26.980
pilots are actually
required to do an auto land.

00:05:26.980 --> 00:05:29.540
- You don't have that visual
output out the window,

00:05:29.540 --> 00:05:31.330
so that actually helps

00:05:31.330 --> 00:05:33.640
but you're again watching it like a hawk

00:05:33.640 --> 00:05:35.150
and at any point in time,

00:05:35.150 --> 00:05:37.550
if it's not flying the
path it's supposed to fly,

00:05:37.550 --> 00:05:38.980
we disconnect it.

00:05:38.980 --> 00:05:41.320
- [Narrator] Automation,
including the autopilot system,

00:05:41.320 --> 00:05:43.610
has contributed to
record low accident rates

00:05:43.610 --> 00:05:44.651
in recent years.

00:05:44.651 --> 00:05:47.520
However, it was a faulty
flight control system,

00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:49.690
combined with pilot
missteps and other factors

00:05:49.690 --> 00:05:51.070
that investigators say led

00:05:51.070 --> 00:05:56.070
to two fatal crashes of Boeing
737 MAX in 2018 and 2019.

00:05:56.300 --> 00:05:57.870
Boeing has since fixed the system

00:05:57.870 --> 00:05:59.230
and revamped pilot training,

00:05:59.230 --> 00:06:00.700
and with the FAA's approval,

00:06:00.700 --> 00:06:03.953
the MAX is now being used
for passenger flights again.

00:06:05.150 --> 00:06:07.520
Meanwhile, airplane
automation is continuing

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:08.560
with companies testing out

00:06:08.560 --> 00:06:10.230
increasingly advanced systems aimed

00:06:10.230 --> 00:06:13.310
at reducing pilot workload
and enhancing safety.

00:06:13.310 --> 00:06:14.890
But for all its advances,

00:06:14.890 --> 00:06:17.540
autopilot still has a lot to learn.

00:06:17.540 --> 00:06:19.430
- The autopilot cannot
maneuver the aircraft

00:06:19.430 --> 00:06:22.430
from the gate to the runway and take off.

00:06:22.430 --> 00:06:23.860
Nor can it maneuver the aircraft

00:06:23.860 --> 00:06:26.550
from the runway back to the gate.

00:06:26.550 --> 00:06:28.060
- [Narrator] For now,
think about autopilot

00:06:28.060 --> 00:06:29.900
as an extra set of hands in the air,

00:06:29.900 --> 00:06:32.317
following Laxo's detailed instructions.

00:06:32.317 --> 00:06:34.900
(lively music)

